course: dictionary
lesson: 12363
title: Croaky voices
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A croaky voice sounds rough, low and hoarse, as if the throat is dry or sore. «Mum sounded croaky on the phone, so I knew she had a cold.» The word croaky tells us her voice was not clear and smooth, and the cold is the clue.
explain: Look at the words around it. «After shouting all afternoon at the football match, Sam's voice was croaky.» Shouting can make a throat sore, so croaky must mean rough and hoarse. Try the swap: "Sam's voice was rough and hoarse" still makes sense, so the meaning fits.
explain: Croaky can also describe a sound like a frog or a crow. «The old crow let out a croaky call from the tree.» Here the clue is the crow in the tree, not a sore throat. The same word can fit different sounds, so check the clues every time.
fact: Croaky is an adjective, so it describes a noun, such as a voice.
fact: Croaky means sounding rough, low and hoarse.
fact: A voice often becomes croaky when you have a sore throat or a cold.
fact: «Croaky» can also mean sounding like the croak of a frog or crow.
fact: The comparative forms are croakier (more croaky) and croakiest (the most croaky).
exercise: «Mum sounded croaky on the phone.» What does croaky mean here? | Happy and loud | *Rough and hoarse, as if she had a cold | Very quick and clear | Far away and tiny
exercise: «The old crow let out a croaky call from the tree.» What kind of sound is croaky here? | Like a bell ringing | Like a crowd cheering | Like a loud drum | *Like the croak of a crow
exercise: «After shouting all afternoon, Sam's voice was croaky.» Find and copy the word that tells you why his voice was croaky. | Sam | voice | afternoon | *shouting
test: Which of these is a common reason a voice becomes croaky? | Skipping in the park | *A sore throat | Reading a comic book
test: What do croakier and croakiest mean? | *More croaky, and the most croaky | Less croaky than before | Like a frog's jump | Quieter and softer
exam: Ella says, «Dad's voice is croaky, so he probably needs a drink of water.» Why might Ella think this? | He is far away on the phone | He is singing a happy song | *His throat may be dry or sore | Croaky means very happy
